{
  "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
  "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral\nargument before the Supreme Court of the United\nStates at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United Student Aid Funds, Inc.",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress has precisely delineated three\ntypes of debt in bankruptcy: those that are\ndischargeable, those that are dischargeable unless the\ncreditor timely objects, and those debts that are simply\nnot dischargeable. Student loans fall within a subset\nof this third category. Their exception from discharge\nis self-executing unless a debtor proves that\nrepayment will cause an undue hardship on the debtor and\nthe debtor's dependents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section 1328 and section 523 are best\nconstrued as self-executing limitations on the effect of\nthe bankruptcy court's discharge order rather than as\ndirectives to the bankruptcy court. There are two\nreasons for --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are a lot of commas.\nWhat comma are you referring to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Wait a moment. What would it be in the case where you have a discharge order and it says things in it which somebody feels fall within 1 of these 13 categories? Now, are you saying that that somebody can come back and make his argument 15 years later, because he will say that, since it falls in that category, the judgment is void insofar as this language covers what I don't want it to cover?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HEYTENS: Well, Justice Breyer, there are three very specific categories of somebodies who can't do that, and Congress has specifically identified those three categories. In 523(c), Congress specifically identified three categories of non-dischargeable debt for which the onus is on the creditor to request a hearing and obtain a determination by the bankruptcy court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: All right. So -- so you\u2019re almost begging the question, because it's possible to argue that if a debt is not dischargeable at all under any circumstance, your argument might have more legs because then the court has no jurisdiction over that property."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Where is (c)? You have been talking about 523(c). I can't find it in any of the materials."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Heytens",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0001-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The logical consequences of affirming the Ninth Circuit's judgment in this case is to tell every single chapter 13 debtor who has a student loan debt to include a provision like this in his plan, in the hopes that the creditor will not object and he will be able to obtain a discharge in the absence of any finding by the bankruptcy court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: I was going to ask whether or not in -- on the facts of this case, the client could have waited until the final judgment, not appeal, but then come in under Rule 60?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Let me just ask this and maybe I have bankruptcy law wrong. My -- my understanding is that if creditors are not listed, they are not discharged, correct? I think that's right in most cases. If you don't list the creditor, the creditor is not discharged."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I guess I don't understand your position, because I thought you had said that this should not have been discharged and now -- now you\u2019ve answered to Justice Ginsburg that so long as the -- as the creditor appears they can all be discharged. Now, which is it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Wanslee",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0003-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. WANSLEE: The controlling order here is the discharge order. And the reason why is because bankruptcy is a continuum of events all leading to the discharge. The discharge is the goal. That's what the debtor wants. But only Congress can tell a debtor what he gets to discharge."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1134",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "cc53f7fbb2bf059dc086796705ddcebca1a0ca206aa1606f26dc0ccbdd2792a5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-cc53f7fbb2bf-0003-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. WANSLEE: Thank you, Your Honor.\n(Whereupon, at 12:06 p.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
